Stars can gravitationally capture ISOs onto bound orbits. Capture requires a change in an ISO’s velocity comparable to its velocity at infinity relative to the Sun – that is, its speed after subtracting off the Sun’s gravitational acceleration. The best-studied ISO capture mechanism is the result of Jupiter (Valtonen & Innanen, 1982; Hands & Dehnen, 2020; Napier et al., 2021a; Dehnen & Hands, 2022). Jupiter’s gravitational influence can temporarily capture passing ISOs, whose orbits remain stably within the confines of the Solar System for millions of years (or more), before being re-ejected (Napier et al., 2021b; Dehnen et al., 2022). Other mechanisms for ISO capture have been studied to some degree – for instance, the Galactic tide has been shown to temporarily capture passing ISOs (Peñarrubia, 2023).

In this paper, we show that stellar flybys are another mechanism for ISO capture – and, unlike other mechanisms, one that does not require the existence of any giant planets. During a stellar encounter, the impulse imparted by the flyby star can lead to the capture of a fraction of ISOs that happen to be passing through a star’s sphere of gravitational influence (defined by the tidal radius – see Eq. 1), and that also have a sufficiently low velocity relative to the Sun. This mechanism is analogous to the capture of the irregular satellites of the giant planets during close encounters suffer
